  "worked_solution": "Let $T$ be the set of all triangles. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in T,$ $x$ is not acute.\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∀x\\in T,\\,x\\text{ is not acute}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in T,\\,x\\text{ is not acute}) & ≡∃x\\in T,\\,¬(x\\text{ is not acute}) \\\\  & ≡∃x\\in T,\\,x\\text{ is acute}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in T$ such that $x$ is acute.\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Some triangles are acute.",
